// D1572 — the same integer `match`, but over an UN-ANNOTATED scrutinee, at every delivery
// position and in both faces. The sibling template (`int-match-expression`) annotates the
// scrutinee; this one is the shape the consumer's own VL-020 probe has, which annotates
// nothing anywhere.
//
// The two are a different question, not a repeat. An un-annotated parameter's type is a HOLE,
// so no vocabulary can be chosen from the scrutinee at all — the ARMS choose, and each one
// defers a `==` constraint the CALL adjudicates. That is what the hand-written if-chain twin
// (`if id == 0x10 { … }`) already did, so a `match` that could not do it would be a capability
// the twin has and this construct does not, at exactly the spelling the consumer writes.
//
// The PROOF discriminates: `tag()` returns 2, so a cell that dispatched on the wrong arm (or
// fell through to `_`) prints something other than `two`.
//
// Format: scripts/capability-probes/matrix.py, whose docstring is the spec.
// @@PRELUDE@@
function tag() { 2 }
function pick(id) {
  match id {
    1 => "one"
    2 => "two"
    _ => "other"
  }
}
function wild(id) {
  match id {
    1 => "one"
    _ => "wild"
  }
}
// @@VALUE@@
pick(tag())
// @@TYPE@@
string
// @@FALLBACK@@
"none"
// @@TEST@@
v == "two"
// @@HIT@@
print("two")
// @@MISS@@
print(v)
// @@WANT@@
two
// @@VALUE2@@
wild(tag())
// @@WANT2@@
wild
